<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfonso II d'Este</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1533–1597</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfonso II d'Este was the last Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">Este family</a>, ruling from 1559 to 1597. His court was a centre of music and literature, home to the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torquato-tasso">Torquato Tasso</a>, whom he confined for seven years as insane, and to the singers of the concerto delle donne. Despite three marriages he left no legitimate heir, and in 1598 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-viii">Pope Clement VIII</a> annexed Ferrara to the Papal States.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-deste.jpg" alt="Alfonso II d'Este" width="320" height="519" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Studio of Cesare Aretusi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cesare_Aretusi_Retrato_de_Alfonso_II_de_Este,_duque_de_Ferrara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
